Article 12

(1) Any person who intentionally fails to provide protection to women and children thereby causing violence, allows the occurrence of violence, and or fails to report and fails to provide protection to victims, shall be subject to sanctions as stipulated in the applicable laws and regulations;

(2) Officials appointed to organize protection who do not carry out their obligations and responsibilities shall be subject to administrative actions or sanctions; (3) PPT managers who carry out service duties that violate service principles may be subject to sanctions in accordance with the internal mechanisms of PPT.

Article 8

(1) Forms of services for victims organized by PPT include a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical and paramedical personnel;

b. medicolegal services constitute a form of medical service for the purposes of evidence in the legal field;

c. psychosocial services constitute services provided by companions in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from threats and intimidation for victims and provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength, and independence in resolving their problems;

d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process; e. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

(2) The service mechanism as referred to in paragraph (1) is organized according to standard operational procedures which will be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 7

(1) The organization of protection for victims is carried out in an integrated manner within the PPT framework;

(2) Provisions concerning PPT will be further regulated in the Regent Regulation.

